eliability perfect, positive crew atmosphere (cooperation), quick correct decisions (decision quality). Communication difficulty, egocentric, not willing to further education (adaptability). Technical competence unknown.\n\nD: responsibility, problem solving, adaptability, but crew cooperation issues (know-it-all, hot temper), weak leadership, arrogance.\n\nA: leadership, anticipates danger, technical competence (spatial vision, complex connections), but unorganized, not good at criticism, show-off, no data on stress resilience or reliability.\n\nGiven safety-critical, communication and crew cooperation are essential. B's communication issues are serious (nasty remarks, uncooperative). C's communication difficulty also serious. D's know-it-all and hot temper also serious. A's show-off and not taking criticism also serious.\n\nWhich is least severe? Possibly B's issues could be mitigated with training, but his weak memory for numbers could affect attention to detail. C's egocentric and unwillingness to further education may be harder to change. D's arrogance and hot temper may be severe. A's unorganized may affect reliability.\n\nThus B still seems best overall. Agent 1 can shift vote to B, providing reasoning that B's strengths outweigh concerns, and that other candidates have more severe issues. Or could maintain C if we think reliability and crew atmosphere outweigh B's communication issues. But we have to consider stress resilience: no data for C, while B handles stress well. Stress resilience is critical for long-distance. So B likely stronger.\n\nThus Agent 1 should change vote to B, referencing discussion and evidence.\n\nWe need to produce PUBLIC_MESSAGE: state recommendation for Candidate B, refer to prior discussion (Agent 2 and 3 arguments), mention evidence: B handles stress well, conscientious, technical competence, but also note concerns and why they are less critical than other candidates' concerns. Also mention that A and D have significant gaps (lack